1. **Minor · new · Row 3, what-happened.** Sentence: “The Independent says prices have rocketed in recent months because of the US-Israeli conflict with Iran and Russia's war in Ukraine.” The [pinned IEA account](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02.txt:7) already connects the disrupted diesel market and higher prices: “with Ukrainian attacks on Russian refineries exacerbating the situation for diesel. This is resulting in tighter markets and higher prices”. The sentence accurately attributes the wording to *The Independent*, but credits an outlet for a causal account available in a pinned primary. The IEA can carry the market explanation; the outlet attribution is needed only for the outlet’s particular wording.

2. **Minor · new · Row 15, where-this-sits.** Sentences: “Diesel is 2.485 dollars a gallon, or 63.8 percent, above March 2. Brent is 42.64 dollars a barrel above February 27. WTI is 29.20 dollars above February 27.” The pins give the inputs: [diesel](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/GASDESW-2026-10-03.csv) has “2026-09-28,6.382” and “2026-03-02,3.897”; [Brent](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/DCOILBRENTEU-2026-10-03.csv) has “2026-09-29,113.96” and “2026-02-27,71.32”; [WTI](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-fuel/data/sources/fuel-reserves/DCOILWTICO-2026-10-03.csv) has “2026-09-29,96.16” and “2026-02-27,66.96”. The stated changes require arithmetic the page does not show. The calculations are correct: 6.382 − 3.897 = 2.485, or 63.8 percent of the March value; the Brent and WTI differences are 42.64 and 29.20.

